    <section>
      <h2 id="overview-and-data-controller">1. Overview and data controller</h2>
      <p>
        AuroFlow Panel is a browser-based application
        for managing virtual machines through third-party
        infrastructure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The application does not operate a central user
        database. Most operations take place directly
        between your browser and third-party APIs.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher identified in the
        <a href={href('/legal/')}>Legal Notice</a>
        is responsible for personal data processing under
        their control.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="information-processed">2. Information processed</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>GitHub username, account information and avatar.</li>
        <li>GitHub OAuth access tokens and granted permissions.</li>
        <li>GitHub repository names, settings and workflow metadata.</li>
        <li>ngrok and Hugging Face access tokens supplied by the user.</li>
        <li>Dataset identifiers and storage metadata.</li>
        <li>Remote Desktop endpoints and network configuration.</li>
        <li>Local application activity and preferences.</li>
        <li>Technical connection information processed by hosting providers.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="authentication-and-permissions">3. Authentication and permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Authentication uses GitHub OAuth Device Flow.
        Your GitHub password is not entered into this application.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Cloudflare Worker relays authentication requests
        and responses between your browser and GitHub.
        The relay does not implement persistent token
        storage or explicit token logging.
      </p>
      <p>
        The application requests the GitHub OAuth scopes
        <code>repo</code> and <code>workflow</code>.
        These permissions are broad and may grant access
        to repositories beyond the selected workspace.
      </p>
      <p>
        Authorization may be revoked through your
        <a href="https://github.com/settings/applications">
          GitHub application settings
        </a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="credential-storage">4. Credential storage</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          GitHub tokens are stored in sessionStorage by
          default, with an application-level inactivity
          limit of eight hours.
        </li>
        <li>
          If you enable persistent sign-in, the GitHub
          token is stored in localStorage and accepted
          by the application for up to seven days.
        </li>
        <li>
          ngrok tokens are encrypted in the browser using
          the repository public key and submitted as
          GitHub Actions secrets.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hugging Face tokens are also submitted as
          encrypted GitHub Actions secrets and may
          optionally be retained in sessionStorage.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Browser storage is not an encrypted credential
        vault. Other scripts running under the same web
        origin may be able to access stored information.
      </p>
      <p>
        Signing out clears locally stored credentials
        but does not revoke tokens or delete credentials
        previously saved in GitHub Actions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="purposes-and-legal-bases">5. Purposes and legal bases</h2>
      <p>
        Information is processed to authenticate users,
        configure workspaces, manage virtual machine
        sessions, display operational information and
        provide persistent storage.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where applicable, processing necessary to provide
        the requested functionality is based on the
        provision of that service. Security-related
        processing may rely on legitimate interests.
        Optional credential retention is based on your
        affirmative choice and may be disabled.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="third-party-services">6. Third-party services</h2>
      <p>
        The application relies on external providers
        that process information under their own terms
        and privacy policies.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">
            GitHub
          </a>: authentication, hosting, repositories,
          workflow execution and secrets.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">
            Cloudflare
          </a>: authentication relay infrastructure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://ngrok.com/privacy">
            ngrok
          </a>: network tunnelling and remote connectivity.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://huggingface.co/privacy">
            Hugging Face
          </a>: virtual machine persistence and dataset management.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These providers may process information outside
        the European Economic Area. International
        transfers are subject to applicable legal
        requirements and provider safeguards.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="virtual-machine-data">7. Virtual machine data</h2>
      <p>
        Virtual machine disk changes and associated
        firmware and TPM state may be stored in a
        Hugging Face dataset under your account.
      </p>
      <p>
        These files may contain personal information,
        credentials, installed software and documents
        created during your virtual machine sessions.
      </p>
      <p>
        A private dataset restricts access but does not
        constitute end-to-end encryption.
      </p>
      <p>
        Public GitHub workspaces may expose connection
        endpoints, workflow metadata and operational logs.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="browser-storage-and-tracking">8. Browser storage and tracking</h2>
      <p>
        The application uses localStorage and
        sessionStorage for authentication, workspace
        preferences, operational state and local activity.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current application does not implement
        advertising trackers or analytics scripts.
        Hosting providers may independently process
        technical connection information.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="data-retention">9. Data retention</h2>
      <p>
        Local workspace preferences and activity records
        remain in browser storage until cleared.
        Credential retention follows the limits described
        above. Expired GitHub credentials are removed
        when the application checks stored credentials.
      </p>
      <p>
        Repository data, Actions secrets, workflow logs
        and virtual machine datasets are retained according
        to your settings and the relevant provider's
        retention policies.
      </p>
      <p>
        Infrastructure log retention is subject to the
        applicable hosting configuration and provider policies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="your-rights">10. Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Where applicable under the GDPR, you may request
        access, rectification, erasure, restriction,
        portability or object to processing. You may
        withdraw consent where processing relies on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        You may clear local application data, revoke
        GitHub authorization, remove Actions secrets
        and delete your persistence dataset.
      </p>
      <p>
        Requests concerning processing under the
        publisher's control may be submitted using the
        contact details in the
        <a href={href('/legal/')}>Legal Notice</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        You also have the right to lodge a complaint with
        the relevant supervisory authority, including
        the French
        <a href="https://www.cnil.fr">CNIL</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="changes-to-this-policy">11. Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        This policy may be updated to reflect changes
        in the application, its infrastructure or
        applicable legal requirements.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="scope-and-separation-of-responsibilities">12. Scope and separation of responsibilities</h2>
      <p>This policy concerns the hosted AuroFlow Panel website and its authentication relay. It does not replace the privacy policies of GitHub, Cloudflare, ngrok, Hugging Face, Microsoft or software installed inside a virtual machine.</p>
      <p>Users control their own workspace repositories, persistence datasets, virtual machine contents and third-party accounts.</p>
      <p>The publisher does not receive administrative access to those resources merely because a user signs in to the panel.</p>
      <p>Depending on the circumstances, users processing personal information inside their virtual machines may have independent data protection obligations.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="information-obtained-from-github">13. Information obtained from GitHub</h2>
      <p>After authentication, the panel retrieves account information required to identify the signed-in user, including the GitHub login and avatar.</p>
      <p>It also retrieves repository information, workflow runs, job statuses, repository variables, secret names, shutdown requests and connection information necessary to manage the selected workspace.</p>
      <p>GitHub Actions secret values cannot be retrieved through the normal secrets API. The panel may check whether a named secret exists, but this does not disclose its stored value.</p>
      <p>The OAuth authorization is broader than the individual workspace. The repo and workflow scopes can authorize operations on other repositories accessible to the authenticated account, subject to GitHub permissions and organization policies.</p>
      <p>The panel is designed to operate on the selected workspace. Users should nevertheless consider the security implications of granting broad OAuth permissions.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="authentication-relay-and-token-transit">14. Authentication relay and token transit</h2>
      <p>GitHub's Device Flow endpoints cannot be called directly from the browser under the required cross-origin configuration. The application therefore uses a Cloudflare Worker as an authentication relay.</p>
      <p>The relay forwards requests to GitHub's device authorization and access token endpoints. During authentication, device codes and the resulting access token pass through the relay.</p>
      <p>The Worker adds the configured OAuth client identifier and requests the required GitHub scopes.</p>
      <p>The current relay implementation does not contain a persistent user database, explicit credential logging or application-level token storage. Authentication responses include a Cache-Control: no-store directive.</p>
      <p>This does not establish that infrastructure-level logging, diagnostic collection or security processing is disabled. Such processing depends on the actual Cloudflare deployment configuration and provider policies.</p>
      <p>The relay's origin restrictions are a browser access control measure and must not be understood as an independent user authentication mechanism.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="detailed-browser-storage-inventory">15. Detailed browser storage inventory</h2>
      <p>The application uses the golden-panel.v2 namespace to organize browser storage.</p>
      <p>The github entry contains the OAuth access token, account login and timestamps used for session restoration. It is stored in sessionStorage by default or localStorage when persistent sign-in is selected.</p>
      <p>The hf entry may contain a Hugging Face access token in sessionStorage.</p>
      <p>The workspace entry contains the selected repository owner and name in localStorage.</p>
      <p>Entries beginning with dispatch. contain timestamps used to reduce duplicate workflow launches across browser tabs.</p>
      <p>Entries beginning with activity. contain local operational messages and timestamps, limited to the latest 60 entries per workspace.</p>
      <p>The cancel entry contains up to ten workflow run identifiers associated with cancellation requests from the current tab.</p>
      <p>A temporary legacyRepo entry may be used to migrate a repository preference from an earlier version of the panel.</p>
      <p>These entries are intended for application functionality rather than advertising, profiling or cross-site behavioral tracking.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="credential-expiration-and-session-restoration">16. Credential expiration and session restoration</h2>
      <p>The default GitHub session storage validity period is eight hours from the stored activity timestamp. Persistent sign-in uses a seven-day validity period measured from the original storage timestamp.</p>
      <p>These limits are checked when stored credentials are read. They are not a guarantee that an already loaded session will terminate at the exact expiration time.</p>
      <p>Expiration of local storage does not revoke the corresponding OAuth token at GitHub.</p>
      <p>Browser session restoration features may also restore sessionStorage after a browser restart. Closing a tab should therefore not be treated as a guaranteed method of credential revocation.</p>
      <p>Users requiring immediate invalidation should revoke the authorization directly through GitHub.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="shared-browser-origins-and-credential-exposure">17. Shared browser origins and credential exposure</h2>
      <p>Browser storage is isolated primarily by web origin, not by URL path.</p>
      <p>Where several applications are hosted under the same GitHub Pages origin or custom domain, scripts executing under that origin may potentially access shared localStorage.</p>
      <p>Namespacing storage keys reduces accidental collisions but does not provide cryptographic isolation.</p>
      <p>Users should avoid persistent sign-in on public, shared or otherwise untrusted computers and should keep their browser and extensions up to date.</p>
      <p>The application cannot protect credentials against every form of browser compromise, malicious extension, operating system compromise or cross-site scripting vulnerability.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="repository-secrets-and-credential-transmission">18. Repository secrets and credential transmission</h2>
      <p>ngrok and Hugging Face tokens entered during workspace configuration are encrypted using the GitHub repository public key before submission to the Actions secrets API.</p>
      <p>The encryption operation takes place in the browser. The ngrok token is not intentionally retained in persistent browser storage.</p>
      <p>The Hugging Face token may additionally be retained in sessionStorage for storage management operations, depending on the selected setting.</p>
      <p>GitHub makes configured secrets available to authorized workflow executions. Consequently, the security of workflow definitions, repository permissions and dependencies remains important.</p>
      <p>Users should grant only the permissions required for their intended operations and rotate credentials if compromise is suspected.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="public-repositories-and-disclosure">19. Public repositories and disclosure</h2>
      <p>New instance repositories are private by default in the standard setup.</p>
      <p>Users may choose to change repository visibility. A public repository may expose its source code, commit history, workflow logs, issues, operational metadata and Remote Desktop connection information.</p>
      <p>Changing a repository back to private does not necessarily remove information previously copied, cached or otherwise obtained by third parties.</p>
      <p>Repository visibility does not determine the privacy of the associated Hugging Face dataset. The persistence dataset must remain private.</p>
      <p>Users should review repository contents and security settings before making any workspace public.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="virtual-machine-contents-and-remote-desktop-traffic">20. Virtual machine contents and remote desktop traffic</h2>
      <p>The panel does not directly render the remote Windows desktop. Remote Desktop traffic is handled through the user's RDP client, the configured network tunnel and the virtual machine.</p>
      <p>The panel polls infrastructure status and workflow metadata, but its current implementation does not monitor keyboard input, screen contents or application usage inside the guest operating system.</p>
      <p>Software running inside Windows may independently collect telemetry, connect to external services or process personal information.</p>
      <p>Disk overlays may contain documents, browser histories, application databases, credentials, deleted-file remnants and other information written to the virtual disk.</p>
      <p>The publisher does not operate a content inspection service for those disk overlays.</p>
      <p>Users should avoid storing sensitive or regulated information unless they have assessed the security and compliance requirements of the complete infrastructure.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="data-retention-and-deletion-procedures">21. Data retention and deletion procedures</h2>
      <p>Signing out removes the panel's locally stored credentials and cancellation records. It does not automatically remove the selected workspace preference or all local activity history.</p>
      <p>Clearing workspace information through Settings removes the relevant local configuration and activity records. It does not delete the remote repository, workflow logs, secrets or persistence dataset.</p>
      <p>Browser site-data controls can be used to remove remaining local application storage.</p>
      <p>GitHub OAuth access must be revoked separately through GitHub application settings. Actions secrets must be removed or replaced in the relevant repository.</p>
      <p>Persistence datasets may be deleted through the available management controls or directly through Hugging Face.</p>
      <p>Deleting a dataset or repository does not establish that every historical backup, provider log, cache or legally retained copy is immediately erased. Provider-specific retention policies remain applicable.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="security-measures-and-limitations">22. Security measures and limitations</h2>
      <p>The application uses HTTPS for supported web and API communications, browser-side encryption for Actions secrets, origin restrictions on the authentication relay and a production Content Security Policy.</p>
      <p>These measures reduce certain risks but do not guarantee absolute security.</p>
      <p>Private repositories and private datasets provide access restrictions, not end-to-end encryption of virtual machine contents.</p>
      <p>The publisher cannot guarantee the security of user devices, third-party infrastructure, browser extensions, installed guest software or modified deployments.</p>
      <p>Suspected vulnerabilities should be reported privately to contact@fox3000foxy.com. Access tokens, passwords, private dataset contents and active RDP endpoints should not be included in public reports.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="support-communications">23. Support communications</h2>
      <p>If you contact the publisher by email, the information you voluntarily provide may be processed to respond to your request, investigate technical problems or handle legal and security matters.</p>
      <p>Such information may include your email address, message contents and relevant technical details.</p>
      <p>Communications should contain only the information necessary to investigate the issue.</p>
      <p>Support correspondence is retained only as necessary for the relevant purpose, subject to applicable legal obligations and legitimate needs to establish or defend legal claims.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="international-processing-and-safeguards">24. International processing and safeguards</h2>
      <p>The infrastructure used by the project may involve processing in countries outside the European Economic Area.</p>
      <p>The location of a user, repository or dataset does not necessarily determine where every request, backup or technical log is processed.</p>
      <p>Applicable transfer safeguards depend on the provider, contractual arrangement, processing activity and destination.</p>
      <p>Where the publisher is responsible for an international transfer of personal information, the applicable requirements of Chapter V of the GDPR must be respected.</p>
      <p>Users may contact the publisher for information about safeguards applicable to processing under the publisher's responsibility.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="automated-decisions-and-user-activity">25. Automated decisions and user activity</h2>
      <p>The panel derives technical virtual machine states from workflow information and uses automated logic to display statuses, prevent duplicate launches and request operational actions.</p>
      <p>The current application does not implement advertising profiles, behavioral scoring or automated decisions intended to produce legal or similarly significant effects concerning individuals.</p>
      <p>The decision to use or shut down an idle virtual machine remains with the end user, subject to workflow time limits and provider restrictions.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 id="exercising-data-protection-rights">26. Exercising data protection rights</h2>
      <p>Privacy requests may be submitted to contact@fox3000foxy.com.</p>
      <p>Please describe the processing concerned and the right you wish to exercise. Additional information may be requested where reasonably necessary to verify identity or locate the relevant information.</p>
      <p>Where the GDPR applies, requests are handled within the applicable statutory time limits, normally one month, subject to permitted extensions.</p>
      <p>The publisher may be unable to access or delete information held exclusively within a user's own account or by an independent provider. In such cases, the user may need to exercise the relevant rights directly with that provider.</p>
      <p>Users must not provide account passwords, OAuth tokens or other secrets as proof of identity.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 id="allocation-of-responsibilities">Allocation of responsibilities and third-party dependencies</h2>
      <p>
        AuroFlow Panel acts as a technical interface between the end user and independently operated third-party services.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher does not control the internal infrastructure, operational decisions, billing practices, retention policies or contractual restrictions of these providers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users are responsible for reviewing the applicable terms, privacy policies, licensing requirements and usage restrictions before using any integrated service.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher provides information and warnings concerning known technical limitations, security risks and third-party dependencies. Such information does not constitute a certification, guarantee of compliance or authorization to use external resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        Users remain responsible for their accounts, credentials, workloads, virtual machine contents, resource consumption and configuration decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publisher remains responsible for its own processing activities, distribution practices and obligations under applicable law.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in this provision excludes or transfers responsibilities that cannot lawfully be excluded or transferred.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
